A||N||5||87GTBro||Help!!  Acceleration Diagnosis needed||||5||
Z||000000||87GTBro||05-27-2000||06:27 PM||||My 86.5 GT 2.8 recently started giving me attitude.  The car stopped starting easily.  It takes 5 minutes and a good 15-20 cranks to get it to finally turn over.  While I am trying to start the engine, the exhaust fumes smell a little like gasoline.<P>But that is not the only symptom my baby has.  When the engine is running and I slam on the gas the engine rpms will decrease about 500 and then increase from there.  Sometimes the engine completely dies when I step on the gas.  I also notice a major decrease in engine power when the A/C is running.<P>I am thinking that it could be the IAC (Sometimes I hear what sounds like the intake slamming shut) or maybe it is my TPS.  Does this sound right?  I don't want to spend money if I'm not exactly sure that is the problem.<P>Oh yeah, I have already changed the spark plugs because that was my first guess.  It didn't help at all.||||reg||5
Z||000001||AL||05-27-2000||06:56 PM||||have you changed the fuel filter? when they get clogged they decrease performance also check if your cold start injector works and check if you have any injector leaking.||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||05-27-2000||09:40 PM||||I'd check for vacuum leaks especially to the MAP. Then, check the TPS. The output at pin B should vary between about .5V and 4.2V.<P>When you turn the key off, the IAC will reset and park. Have someone turn off the engine and then listen for it screwing out. <P>Does opening the throttle a bit with your foot make it start easier? You could have leaking injectors too, but this sounds like a sudden change, right?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||05-28-2000||12:25 AM||||Check your Catalytic converter. If at idle you smell gas (like it's running too rich) then when you accellerate hard  it chokes like you say, It's most likely the cat is clogging up. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierodave||05-28-2000||05:38 AM||||quick check on the cat_______ lay under the car and rap the cat with your palm of hand,<BR>do you hear marbles or something loose it it?<BR>If so i second the bad cat converter<BR>If it all loose in there a chunk of the core will get lodged in the exhaust pipe, and will cause a major<BR>restriction!<BR>if you hear it rattle when banged!<BR>cut the cat off before the elbow<BR>and drop the bolts of the y-pipe<BR>dump out the guts(if they all don't dump out just bust them out with a long rod or tire iron) and install it back on the car with a collar and clamps (for test purposes)<BR>run it, and see how much more power you have. If you find out it's the cat, then please, Run right down and buy a new one and have it installed<BR>as quick as possible to be EPA leagal ( yea right lol :~)<BR>FD<p>[This message has been edited by fierodave (edited 05-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTBro||05-29-2000||11:41 PM||||There is no way my cat could be my problem.  Because my cat is non-existant.  I had a straight pipe put in.  But SHHHH  don't tell the FEDS.<P>I think I fixed the problem though.  I put a fuel pressure tester on the fuel rail and I only had half the pressure that I was supposed to have.  So I looked in the factory service manual and found that I had two possible culprits.  The fuel pump or the fuel pressure regulator.  So, using my head, I realized that the cheapest and easiest choice would be to change the fuel pressure regulator.  I would have a 50/50 chance of fixing the problem.  I swapped it out today.  Man did it make a difference.  Now it drives like it has a V6!!!!  It is amazing how much low fuel pressure affects performance.  Thanks guys for the advice that you gave.||||reg||1
